Introduction
HRTF (Head-Related Transfer Function) is a transfer function which describes how audio in space is heard by human ears, and the relevant data can be stored in SOFA format. The impulse responses for a given test subject can be recorded in miscellaneous directions. The HRTF of an individual is significantly affected by the shapes of their heads, ears, torsos, etc. The purpose of our project is to determine whether there exist significant differences between males and females in impulse response spectra in a fixed direction. We concluded that there are significant differences between male and female in certain intervals of frequencies.
Methodologies
In order to analyze the difference of HRTF data between male and female, we need HRTF datasets. We used HRTF datasets made by RIEC, which include gender tags for certain data. There are 11 female HRTF data and 42 male HRTF data in the RIEC datasets. Therefore, we used these 53 data and excluded the rest.
We used Python for data analysis. We used the package sofar to read the .sofa files. We collected from the front-facing direction and computes the FFT (Fast Fourier Transform) for both left and right ears using scipy package to obtain their magnitude spectra. Since the sample sizes of male and female HRTF data are not equal, we applied Welch’s t-test at each frequency point across the male and female groups, separately for the left and right ears. This test checks whether the means of the two groups differ significantly at each frequency.
After that, we used matplotlib to plot the spectrum-p-value figure. We also recorded all the intervals that has a p-value of less than 0.05, which is our significant value.
